Architecture Summary
VPC across 2 Availability Zones.

2 Public Subnets (for Load Balancer and NAT Gateway).

2 Private Subnets (for application ec2 instances).

Auto Scaling Group to manage EC2 instances in private subnets.

Bastion / Jump Host in a public subnet to securely access private instances via SSH.

Application Load Balancer (ALB) in public subnets to route incoming traffic on Port 80 to Port 8000 on the private instances.

Step 1: Create the VPC Setup (AWS Console)
Go to AWS Console → VPC → Create VPC.

Select "VPC and more" 

Set Name: AWS prod example  

Configuration:

Number of AZs: 2 
Public Subnets: 2  

Private Subnets: 2  
NAT Gateways: 1 per AZ  

VPC Endpoints: None  
Click Create VPC.

Step 2: Create Launch Template & Auto Scaling Group (AWS Console)
Go to EC2 → Launch Templates → Create Launch Template:

Name: AWS prod example  
AMI: Ubuntu  

Instance Type: t2.micro  
Security Group Inbound Rules:

Allow SSH (Port 22) from 0.0.0.0/0  

Allow Custom TCP (Port 8000) from 0.0.0.0/0  
Go to Auto Scaling Groups → Create Auto Scaling Group:

Name: AWS prod example  

Select the Launch Template created above.

Choose the VPC (AWS prod example) and select the 2 Private Subnets 

Set Desired Capacity: 2, Min Capacity: 2, Max Capacity: 2 (or up to 4)  

Review and Click Create Auto Scaling Group.

Step 3: Launch a Bastion Host / Jump Host
Go to EC2 → Launch Instances:

Name: Bastion host  

AMI: Ubuntu  

Instance Type: t2.micro

Key Pair: Select your .pem key (e.g., AWS-login.pem)  

Network Settings: Select the created VPC (AWS prod example) and choose a Public Subnet  

Auto-assign Public IP: Enable  

Security Group: Allow SSH (Port 22) from anywhere  

Click Launch Instance.

Step 4: Copy Key Pair to Bastion Host & Access Private Instance
Execute these commands from your local computer terminal:

1. Securely Copy (SCP) your local .pem key to the Bastion Host  
Bash
scp -i /path/to/your-key.pem /path/to/your-key.pem ubuntu@<BASTION_PUBLIC_IP>:~
(Replace <BASTION_PUBLIC_IP> with the public IP address of your Bastion Host).

2. SSH into the Bastion Host  
Bash
ssh -i /path/to/your-key.pem ubuntu@<BASTION_PUBLIC_IP>
3. Verify the key exists on the Bastion Host  
Bash
ls -l
4. Change file permissions of the .pem key (if required):
Bash
chmod 400 your-key.pem
5. SSH from the Bastion Host into the Private EC2 Instance  
Bash
ssh -i your-key.pem ubuntu@<PRIVATE_INSTANCE_IP>
(Replace <PRIVATE_INSTANCE_IP> with the internal private IP of one of your private instances created by ASG).

Step 5: Deploy the Web Application on the Private Instance
Run these commands inside the private EC2 instance terminal  

Bash
# 1. Create an index.html file
cat << 'EOF' > index.html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<body>
<h1>My first AWS project</h1>
<p>To demonstrate apps in private subnet</p>
</body>
</html>
EOF

# 2. Run Python Simple HTTP Server on Port 8000
python3 -m http.server 8000
(This starts a basic web server serving the HTML file on port 8000)  

Step 6: Create Target Group & Application Load Balancer (AWS Console)
1. Create Target Group  
Go to EC2 → Target Groups → Create Target Group.

Target Type: Instances  

Target Group Name: AWS-prod-example

Protocol & Port: HTTP on Port 8000  

VPC: Choose AWS prod example  

Register targets: Select the two private EC2 instances, click "Include as pending below", and click Create Target Group [[39:24]].

2. Create Application Load Balancer (ALB)  
Go to EC2 → Load Balancers → Create Load Balancer → Application Load Balancer.

Name: AWS-prod-example  
Scheme: Internet-facing  

VPC: Select AWS prod example

Mappings: Select both Availability Zones and assign Public Subnets to both  

Listeners and Routing: Set Listener to HTTP : Port 80 and forward to the Target Group created above  

Click Create Load Balancer.

3. Update Load Balancer Security Group  
Go to EC2 → Security Groups (for the Security Group attached to the ALB).

Edit Inbound Rules: Add HTTP (Port 80) from source 0.0.0.0/0  

Step 7: Verify Access
Copy the DNS Name of the Application Load Balancer from the EC2 Console  

Open your web browser and paste the DNS name:

Plaintext
http://<ALB-DNS-NAME>




How to Fix (Step-by-Step)
Step 1: Run SCP correctly from your Windows terminal
Open Command Prompt / PowerShell on your local machine and run the following command (notice the :~ at the end):

DOS
scp -i "C:\Users\.............\aws-login.pem" "C:\Users\.............\aws-login.pem" ubuntu@ec2 public ip:~

Step 2: SSH into the Bastion Host
From the same Windows terminal:

DOS
ssh -i C:\Users\............\aws-login.pem ubuntu@ec2 public ip
Step 3: Fix File Permissions on the Bastion Host
Once logged in as ubuntu@ check that the file is present and set the correct permissions:

Bash
# Verify the key file is present
ls -l

# Set secure read-only permissions for the key
chmod 400 aws-login.pem
Step 4: SSH into your Private Instance
Now connect to your private EC2 instance using the full key name (aws-login.pem):

Bash
ssh -i aws-login.pem ubuntu@private subnet priavte ip



: Kill the process using the port (Recommended)
Run the following commands on your private EC2 instance to find and kill the running process:

Bash
# 1. Identify the process ID (PID) running on port 8000
sudo lsof -i :8000

# OR using fuser:
sudo fuser 8000/tcp
To stop or kill the process blocking port 8000:

Bash
# Kill the process running on port 8000 directly
sudo fuser -k 8000/tcp
Once killed, re-run your web server:

Bash
python3 -m http.server 8000
Option 2: Run the server in the background (nohup)
If you want the web server to keep running even after closing your SSH terminal session, launch it with nohup:

Bash
nohup python3 -m http.server 8000 > server.log 2>&1 &
Option 3: Run on a different port
If you don't want to kill the existing process, start the server on a different port (e.g., 8080):

Bash
python3 -m http.server 8080


 

